<h2>The provision and its logic</h2>
<p>Order XXV Rule 1(1) states the power: at any stage of a suit, the court may, either of its own motion or on the application of any defendant, order the plaintiff, for reasons to be recorded, to give within the time fixed by it security for the payment of all costs incurred and likely to be incurred by any defendant. The proviso then identifies the paradigm case — it directs that such an order <em>shall</em> be made in all cases in which it appears to the court that a sole plaintiff is, or all the plaintiffs are, residing out of India and do not possess sufficient immovable property within India other than the property in suit.</p>
<p>The logic is symmetry. The defendant has no choice about being sued; the least the process can do is ensure that a successful defence is not a pyrrhic one. Security for costs does not prejudge the claim — it prices the plaintiff&rsquo;s access to the defendant&rsquo;s time at the value of the costs the plaintiff would owe on losing.</p>

<h2>How the application works</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Move early.</strong> The application is maintainable at any stage, but its equities are strongest before the defendant has incurred the bulk of its costs — typically with or soon after the written statement.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Prove the foundation.</strong> Foreign residence, absence of Indian immovable property beyond the suit property, or the indicators of a judgment-proof plaintiff: pleadings, public records and the plaintiff&rsquo;s own documents usually suffice.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Quantify sensibly.</strong> Courts fix security by reference to realistic defence costs — court fees on counterclaims aside, counsel&rsquo;s fees and litigation expenses over the likely life of the suit — not speculative maximums.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Default has teeth.</strong> If security is not furnished within the time fixed, Rule 2 mandates dismissal, subject to withdrawal with the court&rsquo;s permission and to restoration on sufficient cause shown.</div>
</div>

<h2>Where it earns its keep today</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Cross-border claims.</strong> Foreign entities suing Indian parties with no Indian asset base beyond the claim itself — the proviso&rsquo;s textbook case.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Shell-company plaintiffs.</strong> Suits by thinly capitalised vehicles whose promoters stand behind the claim but not behind the costs.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Serial litigation.</strong> Plaintiffs with a record of abandoned or dismissed suits against the same defendant, where costs orders have gone unsatisfied.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>NRI property disputes.</strong> Claims to Indian property by overseas plaintiffs whose only Indian asset is the property in suit — which the proviso expressly excludes from the sufficiency reckoning.</div>
</div>

<h2>Strategic cautions</h2>
<p>Three cautions keep the tool sharp. First, do not over-ask: an inflated security demand reads as an attempt to stifle the suit and invites rejection of the application altogether. Second, remember the court&rsquo;s countervailing concern for access to justice; the application should rest on the plaintiff&rsquo;s asset geography and conduct, never on mere poverty. Third, the order protects costs, not the claim — a defendant who wins a security order has secured its expenses, and nothing more; the merits battle remains to be fought and won on its own terms.</p>
<div class="note">This explainer states the general position under the Code of Civil Procedure for information. Whether security for costs is appropriate in any suit depends on its parties, pleadings and forum.</div>
